  "answer": "The supplied passages answer the divisional part, but not the late costs filing part.\n\n**Splitting off goods — Manual practice**\n• Lodge a divisional application identifying both the goods/services being split off and those remaining in the parent. The Manual requires the same applicant and trade mark, a single parent application, and a divisional covering ‘some only’ of the parent’s goods/services. [TMM/Part12/3#1]\n• File while the parent is still pending and before the goods being split off are deleted from it. ‘Pending’ includes accepted and opposed applications. If deletion submissions also state that a divisional will be filed, the Manual says to delay the deletion or amendment until that filing. [TMM/Part12/2#1; TMM/Part12/x-annex-a1-divisional-checklist-for-divisional-applications-filed-on-or-after-27-march-2007#1]\n• Avoid duplication between the divisional goods and those specified to remain in the parent, including overlap through broader wording. The checklist provides for restrictions to eliminate duplication. Once the divisional is valid and complete and there is no such overlap, the examiner is to process the parent’s amendment as soon as practicable. [TMM/Part12/x-annex-a1-divisional-checklist-for-divisional-applications-filed-on-or-after-27-march-2007#1]\n• If you want to carry across the parent’s convention priority claim, the Manual says to make that claim when filing the divisional or within two working days afterwards; otherwise it identifies the parent’s filing date as the divisional’s priority date. [TMM/Part12/5#1]\n\n**Late costs filing — not answered by the supplied material**\nNo supplied passage addresses costs filings, their deadlines or any procedure for a late filing. I therefore cannot specify what to lodge or when for that part.\n\n**Source status:** The procedural guidance above is Manual practice. Although the Manual refers to sections 45–46 and regulation 4.5, those legislative provisions have not been supplied, so